Definition 3.1 of Scott (1981, PRG-19) constructs the product system, whose neighbourhoods are the product neighbourhoods of a neighbourhood of the first system and a neighbourhood of the second. The master is the product of the masters, and it is nonempty because the first master is nonempty. A consistent intersection splits, by the componentwise intersection and inclusion laws, into the intersections of the factors. Every neighbourhood is contained in the master because each factor is contained in its master.
